  "query_preview": "..(Tuesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 456 AM EDT MON AUG 17 2026\n\nDry weather will persist for most of Wednesday as surface high \npressure translates through the Central Appalachians. In its wake,\nan upper level trough will be digging across the Great Lakes and\nsupporting a cold front sagging across the Ohio Valley. By \nWednesday evening, the front will approach eastern Kentucky and \nrain chances will return to the JKL CWA. Of note, some models \ndepict an additional shortwave traversing the base of the trough \n(roughly centered over the Ohio Valley) around the time frontally \nforced convection approaches, or perhaps slightly before. This \nenhanced mid level flow may elevate shear somewhat, and depending \non frontal timing, instability may also be adequate for stronger \nstorms, although model spread remains quite high at this time. Any\nrisk for severe weather (or lack thereof) remains dependent on \nhow these ingredients line up, with overall risk looking quite low\nat this time. Meanwhile, a moist air mass will be in place ahead \nof the front, with LREF mean PW values generally in the range of \n1.6-1.7 in (roughly 80th percentile or higher compared to \nclimatology). This, in addition to potentially enhanced convective\ncoverage if shortwave energy interacts with the front, could \nallow for at least isolated instances of flash flooding. Again, \nmodel variability remains very high at this stage, but WPC has \nalready outlined northwestern portions of JKL area in a Day 3 \nMarginal (Level 1/4) Risk for Excessive Rainfall on Wednesday, and\nan additional Day 4 Marginal Risk on Thursday as the system sags \nsouth. Guidance does suggest the front will slow as it passes \nthrough the southern portion of our area and into Tennessee. \nExactly how quickly this boundary progresses is still a source of \nuncertainty, but rain chances have been maintained through \nThursday to account for this slowing motion. Any hydrological \nconcerns will also be dependent on the speed of this frontal \npassage in addition to the extent of convective coverage.\n\nFollowing the frontal passage, troughing will continue to \namplify, eventually digging all the way into the Southeast as \nridging retreats towards the Four Corners region. This will usher \nin cooler weather, with high temperatures as low as the upper 70s \npossible for some locations on Friday. Additional impulses will \nkeep at least scattered showers and storms in the forecast each \nday, with rain chances generally peaking in the afternoon/evening.",
